  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Catathelasma ventricosum is a distinctive gill fungus belonging to the Biannulariaceae family, characterized by its robust fruiting bodies and unique structural features. This species is found in forested regions of New England where it plays a role in the woodland ecosystem.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Double Ring on Stalk",
      "description": "Features two distinct annuli (rings) on the stalk, which is the defining characteristic of the Biannulariaceae family and this species in particular",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Bulbous Base",
      "description": "The base of the fruiting body is characteristically swollen or bulbous, giving the mushroom a distinctive silhouette",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gill Attachment",
      "description": "Gills are attached directly to the stalk and may appear crowded, with a whitish to pale color",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Catathelasma ventricosum is typically found in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests throughout New England, often fruiting in summer through early fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The double-ringed stalk of Catathelasma ventricosum is thought to be a remnant of the partial veil that covered the gills during development, making it a distinctive feature among New England fungi.",
